From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Noxwell <beebono@yahoo.com>
Date: Tue, 4 Aug 2026 21:44:26 -0500
Subject: [PATCH 202/225] arm64: dts: ums512: give every cpu an idle-injection
 cooling device

The idle-injection cooling that stands in for the inert vendor hw dvfs
was declared as a single thermal-idle node under CPU0, on the assumption
that cpuidle_cooling_register() injects across the whole cpuidle driver
cpumask and so one node covers every cpu it serves.

That is not how psci registers cpuidle. cpuidle-psci.c sets
drv->cpumask = cpumask_of(cpu), i.e. one cpuidle driver per cpu, and
cpuidle_cooling_register() hands that mask straight to
idle_inject_register(). A thermal-idle node therefore covers exactly the
cpu it hangs off. Only cpu0 was ever throttled - measured with a pinned
busy loop at inject=80: cpu0 2102 -> 10645ms, while cpu3/6/7 did not
move at all.

With seven of eight cores unthrottleable, and the cpufreq cooling
devices inert because the hw dvfs block never applies a latched index,
the die had no working control path at all. Under sustained 8-way load
it climbed 95 -> 98 -> 101C and was still rising towards the 110C
critical trip with cooling already pinned at its cap.

Add a thermal-idle node to each cpu and reference the whole cluster from
each of that cluster's zones - throttling only the hot core just
migrates the load. Same load now peaks at 75.1C and regulates to a
steady 70-71C at the passive trip.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
